welcome back to drew's news this year  marks the 20th anniversary of the  september 11th attacks and as we look  back and honor those who lost their  lives on that day there is one man from  boston who is on a very personal journey  in their honor his name is paulie venito  and he's a former united airlines flight  attendant who was off that morning  but lost members of his flight crew and  this year paulie is walking from boston  logan airport  where two of the planes that were  hijacked departed from to ground zero  pushing a beverage cart in honor of his  fellow heroes  he is 50 miles into his 220 mile trek so  we are very happy to speak with him  today where he is please welcome coming  to us from eastern connecticut paulie  veneto  hi polly  wow  hi paulie  how are you good to see you honored to  meet you you are doing such a beautiful  undertaking and  i would love for you to tell us about  why you are doing this  well um  because it's long overdue that we  let this country know in the world know  the horror works of those crew members  on that morning  they were they were never recognized as  the first first responders of 9 11.  they um they went hand-to-hand combat  that morning  and it's understandable why it was  overshadowed because of the enormity of  that day  but um  you know  it's long overdue that this country  recognized true american heroes those  flight attendants up there  they accomplish something that  is mind-boggling to me i get emotional  when i talk about it because every time  i get to this pot i start to feel like  i'm walking in their steps  of what they were thinking what they  were doing under those conditions it was  horrific  but we all know from the phone calls  that they made to the ground that they  were able to let us know where those  terrorists are seated in the midst of  that gruesome scene at eight o'clock in  the morning they had people you know  screaming for help or whatever was did  on the chaos they were still calm voices  and uh there were people just like every  one of us and um not trained to go hand  in hand combat  against terrorism but they did it all  the way to the end so as as a country of  americans we should  you know recognize them  to give their families a little peace  and uh i think of their kids their  nieces and nephews their cousins their  brothers their sisters have never heard  the words  i never heard it  i never heard anybody say  my relative was a flight attendant on 9  11.  and they were here  paulie i want to thank you for being  such an extraordinary  storyteller because the way you just  described  everything  humanizes it for us in a way that others  are not so eloquent  and  able to bring us into the moment of what  those people went through  it's the 20th anniversary of 9 11 and we  tend to look at the macro  but what you are doing is bringing us  into the micro in ways that i find  so compelling  so honoring  and so extraordinary  and i thank you for what you are doing  and i know it's also the sixth  anniversary  for something very big in your personal  life and i was wondering if you could  share that with us as well  absolutely um  after 9 11 i fell into an opiate  addiction  that lasted almost 15 years i flew for  10 years  numbing myself out on pain medication  and  i knew nothing about pain medication but  it almost took my life  um  i was told a long time ago don't quit  five minutes before the miracle  and um  i know that five days after 9 11 i knew  that these guys weren't going to be  recognized  and i i put a picture of these crew  members of 175 on my flight bag and i  brought that with me everywhere  as years went by and as my  addiction  became um  you know uh  you know you get deeper into it deeper  into it and um  i i was i was fueled every year by the  anniversary we come up and i and i  couldn't understand why nobody was  recognizing these people  and um  so in 2011 is when i pretty much crashed  and burned and um  i  i knew that through that time that dark  place i was in all i thought about is i  need to recognize these crew members but  i knew that i couldn't do that i didn't  know if i was going to survive what i  was in to do it i had to i had to  survive to recognize him so i just hung  on i just hung on i just hung on and i  didn't quit five minutes before the  miracle and the miracle was i finally  was able to put the stuff down and the  obsession was lifted it was a miracle  paulie i just see you sitting there on a  miracle shirt and i'm thinking about  your journey and how miraculous it has  been and i'm not talking about the  journey we're seeing now as you walk  across the country i'm talking about  your personal one but when i look at you  walking across the country pushing that  cart i think about how are you doing it  are you going from point a to point b  are you making stops along the way  well  i am um i'm doing it a step at a time a  day at a time a minute at a time  i go as long as my body will let me go  i've trained for this i get mentally  trained for it i've physically trained  for it so  um  i stay in a hotel every night um but i  you know whatever the weather is i i'm  prepared for it i just i do you know  there's a lot a lot my training has  changed a little bit since i started  because i i didn't have people coming  out of their houses and stopping and  pulling up and fire departments  you know and police departments and  everybody's stopping me it's and i'm so  grateful for that it's it's it's turned  into something i never expected the  awareness that you're raising  for this and also you're raising money  too for a non-profit called tuesday's  children in new york can you tell us  about that  well as  that all that part of it the raise and  money part of it is  i'm doing that to my best friend's  sister kelly wilson and and she's in  charge of the foundation which is hers  which is the  powerforward25.com which helps people in  recovery find sober living and grants in  tuesday's children which helps people  from the 911 foundations  and uh  so  they take care of all that stuff all i  care about is the rest of this country  saying you know something let's let the  everybody know let these family members  know that we recognize what they their  family member did that day as first  first responders we recognize that they  were heroes i want every one of those  family members relatives to hear this  from all of us i just i'm just the guy  pushing the card that's that's all it is  i want the rest of the country and the  rest of the world to spread this message  so that it'll reach these family members  or these crew members so their kids and  their nieces and their nephews can  finally say listen my aunt was a flight  attendant on united are american  and and they were a hero i want them to  hear that i think they will thanks to  you paulie and um  you have taken a very personal  journey  that you actually turned outward  and that's why kids are coming out with  signs and why we're talking here on the  show  and we're so honored that you've made a  pit stop with us we are one of many who  are cheering you on and we'd like to  donate five thousand dollars to your  journey  and to keep in recognition our hearts  are with all the brave people that we  lost in the attacks and thank you so  much for describing and bringing to  light their stories their humanity  and go to our website now to find out  how you can support paulie's push right  now paulie  thank you thank you paulie and go polly  go  we'll be right back  thank you paulie thank you so much
 

